#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d43b3e6d-b73a-5814-852b-fd6910b29bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332146} {:answer/id #uuid "87f19102-4df1-52f8-b58a-f73c5c67901c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332147} {:answer/id #uuid "16e123d2-23a7-5390-9302-77d8719ca933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332148} {:answer/id #uuid "6175c781-349c-5a15-8ba5-d768492675fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332149} {:answer/id #uuid "68866aea-92bb-5b43-a8a0-03e92d95f059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87f19102-4df1-52f8-b58a-f73c5c67901c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332147} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae52fd74-1018-55c2-ac7b-11a61a0463e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc429d1e-7f4f-50b6-bac7-a69f2082aa23" :question/math-academy-id "q-334452" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+p-3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+p-3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+1=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+p-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+p-3 \\\\  & =(-1)p+p-3 \\\\  & =-p+p-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-3.\n$$"}]}
